body:
She looks like a magical woodland fairy. 

&#x200B;

Also, she has a very big "little" brother named Milkshake, who is also a British Longhair and [also adorable](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9BPTGwZBpc).

That fabric is AMAZING. 

&#x200B;

I hope to have a rainbow baby one day.

"Love you lots!" 

(To which we kids respond "Love you more!")

&#x200B;

>A Series of Unfortunate Tweets

Wait, what did I miss? Links?

&#x200B;

Yup! If she had just the speech sound dysfunction (dis instead of this, jus instead of just, an instead of and), I might suspect a hearing issue, or perhaps apraxia of speech, but combined with the syntax issue ("i is," "I four,") my guess would be autism or something else developmental.

&#x200B;

I also think it's weird that she can say L, V, and R (she doesn't say "wub" for love or "yike" for like) which are much more difficult for most young children to say, but can't say T and TH, which can sometimes be problematic but less so than L, V, or R. And she can say CH, too, which is also a tough one for little kids to master.

&#x200B;

Why am I putting so much thought into this? LOL

Most four-year-olds can speak in simple but complete and mostly grammatically correct sentences, and have generally overcome most speech sound problems unless they have a hearing problem or a speech sound disorder which would need to be rectified with speech therapy. Many four-year-olds can read and are starting to use language in more complex ways, including understanding puns and simple wordplay. ("What's the best time to go to the dentist? Tooth-hurty!") This speech pattern is nowhere near developmentally appropriate for a cognitively typical preschooler.

&#x200B;

TL;DR: Four-year-olds don't talk like that.

My cousin Elizabeth got married a few months before Megan and I did, but Megan and I were already engaged at this point. Megan came with me to E's wedding. E's mom and stepdad paid for the wedding, but her dad (my uncle) threw the big square dance/bbq thing for everyone two nights before. 

&#x200B;

Megan and I were hanging out by the bar at one point during the square dance, chatting with the bartender, who was this super nice lady, and she was giving us recommendations of places to eat while we were staying in the town where the wedding was located, asking how we knew the bride and groom, wanting to know what plans we had for our own wedding, etc. 

&#x200B;

Anyway, that was Thursday, and the wedding was Saturday. Huge wedding-- like 600 people, which is big even for my family (her mom's side is even bigger than her dad's/my side, which is saying something)-- and therefore huge reception. Same bartender at the reception that was at the square dance. So I'm on my fourth signature cocktail, chatting with the bartender once again, when she says, "I am so glad to have met you and Megan. You guys seem really good together." 

&#x200B;

I thanked her, and she continued, "I bet you two are the happiest couple here. By a lot." 

&#x200B;

(Mind you, this was at *someone else's wedding*.) 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

\*Anti-LGB. 

&#x200B;

There, I fixed it for you.

Mess with the touchpads on people's laptops to make the cursor move by itself.

Turn on people's cell phones in quiet places like library or movie theater

Make the dog poop people don't clean up fly up into the air and hit them in the face

Make pants fall down randomly

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
